a day after the u.s authorized second  booster shots for those over age 50  president joe biden rolled up his sleeve  and made a fresh pitch to americans  we're now in a new moment in this  pandemic  does not mean that covet 19 is over  it means that covet 19 no longer  controls our lives that's what it means  fourth shots are now seen as the best  defense for older and more vulnerable  people in the u.s they're rolling out at  a confusing time case numbers have  plummeted hospitalizations are at their  lowest point in two years it's hard to  tell people that doing something now is  really going to help you  weeks months  maybe even years down the line  there's reason for concern the omicron  ba2 subvariant is now dominant in the  u.s and cases are starting to tick back  up yet millions remain vulnerable  undeterred by the nearly 1 million  pandemic deaths to date today less than  70 percent of eligible americans are  fully vaccinated with two doses and only  46.5 percent have received a booster  dose this really does beg the question  where should we focus our limited  resources is it on providing fourth  doses or is it on other measures for  example making sure we vaccinate people  who may not have even had a single dose  yet  at the same time the once world-leading  u.s response has effectively run dry  congress has not approved funding to  cover the cost of future covid testing  treatment or vaccine doses congress has  to provide the funding america needs to  continue to fight cover 19. yet it may  be tough to convince americans to keep  up the fight  two years in and during a welcome  reprieve in cases many feel like they're  done with the pandemic even if it's not  done with them  jackson prosko global news washington
 

